editorialMd:
Build the result list

head is the first node of a linked list. A node holds a number in val and a link to the next node in next. Follow next until there are no more nodes. Record each val in order. The list has no loops. An empty list gives an empty result list.

Return the answer

Add all numbers in the result list. Return the total. An empty list has a total of 0.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the number of nodes. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function chainInspectionTotalReceipt(head, k) {
  const a=head; let out=[];
  for(let node=a;node;node=node.next)out.push(node.val);
  return out.reduce((s,x)=>s+x,0);
}


typescript
type ListNode = { val: number; next: ListNode | null };
function chainInspectionTotalReceipt(head: ListNode | null, k: number): number {
  const a=head; let out:number[]=[];
  for(let node=a;node;node=node.next)out.push(node.val);
  return out.reduce((s,x)=>s+x,0);
}


